5. Python Frameworks and Libraries
PySide6

PySide6 is the primary desktop GUI framework.

It provides the Qt/Python interface used for:

Main application window
Layouts
Buttons
Menus
Popup windows
Chart containers
User interaction
QWebEngine integration
QWebChannel integration

Major Qt components used include:

QtWidgets
QtCore
QtGui
QtWebEngineWidgets
QtWebChannel
Qt WebEngine

Qt WebEngine provides the Chromium-based browser engine used to render the HTML/JavaScript trading charts inside the desktop application.

Architecture:

PySide6 Application
      ↓
QWebEngineView
      ↓
Chromium/Web Content
      ↓
HTML + JavaScript
      ↓
Lightweight Charts


This allows the application to combine a native desktop GUI with a modern browser-based charting engine.

QWebChannel

QWebChannel provides communication between the Python/Qt application and JavaScript running inside QWebEngine.

It is used for communication such as:

Python
  ↕
JavaScript


This communication is important for:

Crosshair synchronization
Chart state communication
Mouse events
Chart commands
Main dashboard ↔ popup coordination
Pandas

Pandas is used for market-data processing.

It provides:

DataFrame management
Excel data processing
Timestamp handling
Sorting
Data validation
Timeframe aggregation
OHLC calculations

The application uses Pandas as the main in-memory market-data processing layer.

OpenPyXL

OpenPyXL is used as the Excel engine for reading .xlsx market-data files through Pandas.

The project uses Excel workbooks as the current market-data source.

Typical flow:

Excel .xlsx
     ↓
Pandas
     ↓
OpenPyXL engine
     ↓
DataFrame
     ↓
Trading Dashboard

6. JavaScript Charting Library
Lightweight Charts

Lightweight Charts is the primary charting library used by the application.

It provides:

Candlestick charts
Time scale
Price scale
Crosshair
Zooming
Scrolling
Chart interaction

The application adds its own drawing layer on top of Lightweight Charts using HTML Canvas.

Architecture:

Lightweight Charts
       +
HTML Canvas Drawing Layer


This allows the project to provide both standard chart functionality and custom trading drawings.

7. HTML Canvas

HTML Canvas is used as a custom drawing layer above the Lightweight Charts chart.

It is used for:

Trend lines
Horizontal lines
Vertical lines
Rectangles
Drawing selection
Drawing movement
Drawing resizing
Drawing rendering

The drawings are represented using market coordinates such as:

Timestamp
Price

and converted to screen coordinates when rendered.

8. Data Format
Microsoft Excel XLSX

The current Version 1 market-data source is Excel .xlsx files.

The expected OHLC data contains:

Date
Time
Open
High
Low
Close

The application combines Date and Time into a market timestamp.

9. Python Virtual Environment

A Python virtual environment is used during development.

Typical environment:

.venv/


The virtual environment contains the Python dependencies required to run the application.

The .venv directory should NOT be treated as source code and should not be uploaded to the Version 1 GitHub archive.

The environment should instead be recreated from the project's dependency file.

10. Python Dependency Management

The project uses a Python requirements file to record application dependencies.

Primary dependency categories include:

PySide6
Pandas
OpenPyXL

The exact installed package versions should be recorded in a dependency lock/requirements file before major future development.

Recommended command:

pip freeze > requirements-lock.txt


This should be performed from the active project virtual environment.

The generated requirements-lock.txt should be preserved with the Version 1 archive.

